Cap the RDRAM probe at the 8 MiB the console can actually address.

rdram_init() grows total_memory by 2 MiB per chip and stops only when a
chip fails to come up. On real hardware that terminates on its own: an N64
has at most four 2 MiB chips (4 MiB stock, 8 MiB with the Expansion Pak).

On an emulator whose RDRAM register model keeps answering for chips that
are not there, nothing stops it. mupen64plus 2.5.9 is such an emulator: the
probe walks to its structural maximum and reports 64 MB, mupen compares that
against the 8 MB it configured, and refuses to run the ROM.

The cap is not a workaround bolted on from outside -- it is the limit the
very next statement in this function already assumes:

    // This register contains a bitmask that indicates which RDRAM chips are
    // multibank (aka 2 MiB). The bitmask has only 4 bits, so we can only
    // configure 4 chips (which is enough for 4x2MiB = 8MiB total RDRAM).
    int refresh_multibanks = (1 << (chip_id >> 1)) - 1;

Past four chips that shift overflows the 4-bit field, so a probe that counts
further has already left the contract RI_REFRESH is written against. On
hardware the loop never reaches the cap and behaviour is byte-identical.

--- a/boot/rdram.c
+++ b/boot/rdram.c
@@
         // The chip has been configured and mapped. Now go to the next chip.
         // 2 MiB chips must be mapped at even addresses (as they cover two
         // 1-MiB areas), so increase chip-id by 2
         chip_id += 2;
         total_memory += 2*1024*1024;
+
+        // The console cannot address more than 8 MiB, and RI_REFRESH's
+        // multibank bitmask below is only 4 bits wide, so four 2 MiB chips is
+        // the most this function can describe. Stop there rather than trusting
+        // a chip-present answer that no real machine can give.
+        if (total_memory >= 8*1024*1024)
+            break;
     }
